      "hints": [
        "Equate $-\\frac{2}{x} = ax + 3a \\implies ax^2 + 3ax + 2 = 0$.",
        "Set discriminant $\\Delta = (3a)^2 - 4(a)(2) > 0 \\implies a(9a - 8) > 0$.",
        "State $a < 0$ or $a > \\frac{8}{9}$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Equate the curve and line equations:\n$$-\\frac{2}{x} = ax + 3a \\implies -2 = ax^2 + 3ax \\implies ax^2 + 3ax + 2 = 0$$",
        "For the line and curve to intersect at two distinct points, the quadratic must have two distinct real roots, requiring $\\Delta > 0$:\n$$\\Delta = (3a)^2 - 4(a)(2) > 0 \\implies 9a^2 - 8a > 0$$",
        "Factorise and state the solution set:\n$$a(9a - 8) > 0$$\n$$a < 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad a > \\frac{8}{9} \\quad \\left(\\text{or } (-\\infty, 0) \\cup \\left(\\frac{8}{9}, \\infty\\right)\\right)$$"
      ],
